<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  test_room_doors.sdf : a room with two DOORWAYS, one per case of the semantic
  door detector (f1tenth_costmap/semantic_doors.py).

  WHY THIS WORLD EXISTS. semantic_doors.py finds an opening in two shapes, and
  until now neither had a sim scene. CASE A is two collinear walls with a
  door-wide gap between them; CASE B is one wall's END measured
  perpendicularly onto another wall's PLANE. test_semantic_doors.py encodes
  both synthetically; this world is the same geometry in Gazebo, so a sim run
  and the unit tests check the same thing and a disagreement localises itself.

  NOTE ON XML COMMENTS: a literal double hyphen is illegal inside one, which
  is why the rules below are drawn with equals signs (CLAUDE.md, Packaging).

  GAZEBO HARMONIC PLUGIN NAMES (gz-sim-*-system, gz::sim::systems::*), NOT the
  ignition-gazebo-* names its sibling empty_room.sdf still uses. Harmonic
  (gz-sim8 + ros_gz_sim) is what is installed here; ros_ign_gazebo, which
  Fortress needs and which sim_bringup.launch.py still imports, is not. Same
  convention as worlds/test_room_6x6.sdf on the `jazzy` line, which is the
  working Gazebo sim. See output/doors/jazzy_gap.md.

  THIS PACKAGE DOES NOT BUILD ON THIS BRANCH YET. src/f1tenth_sim/COLCON_IGNORE
  has been in place since 3d5ee896: the Gazebo Harmonic port is deferred to
  Item 7 of output/jazzy_migration_plan.md (ros_ign_gazebo to ros_gz_sim,
  ign_ros2_control to gz_ros2_control, bridge types @ignition.msgs.* to
  @gz.msgs.*). So sim_bringup.launch.py, and with it `world:=test_room_doors`,
  cannot start until that happens. This world is written for the port rather
  than for what the launch file does today, and it already runs on its own:
      gz sim -r worlds/test_room_doors.sdf
  which is how its geometry was checked, with no robot and no ROS.

  THE LAYOUT, in map coordinates. The car spawns at the origin facing +x.

       y
       ^                          OUT_py  (y = +6)
     6 +=========================================+
       |                                         |
       |                   | A2   x = 2.5        |
     2 |                   | y in [0.45, 2.0]    |
       |                   |                     |
       |     CAR           :  <  DOOR A, 0.90 m  |  OUT_px
     0 |     (0,0) ====>   :     centre (2.5, 0) |  (x = +7)
       |     2.5 m         |                     |
       |                   | A1                  |
    -3 |                   | y in [-3.0, -0.45]  |
       |                                         |
    -4 |  ========= B1 ====+  : <  DOOR B, 0.80 m|
       |  x in [-2.0, 1.0]    :                  |
       |  y = -4           B2 | x = 1.85         |
    -6 +=========================================+
                                OUT_ny  (y = -6)
         -5                 2.5                7   > x

  DOOR A (case A). A1 and A2 are one line (x = 2.5) with a 0.90 m gap between
  y = -0.45 and y = +0.45. Dead ahead of the spawn at 2.50 m, so it is the
  NEAREST confirmed door and the one missions/enter_nearest_door.json picks.
  The room behind it runs to x = 7.

  DOOR B (case B). B1 runs along x at y = -4.0 and ENDS at (1.0, -4.0); B2 is
  perpendicular to it at x = 1.85. The perpendicular from B1's end onto B2's
  plane is 0.80 m, and its foot sits about 1.0 m inside B2's extent, which is
  what makes this an opening and not a corner (semantic_doors' foot_inset_m).
  About 4.2 m from the spawn, bearing 71 deg to starboard. The room behind it
  runs to y = -6.

  THE 0.80 m IS THE GAP THE LIDAR MEASURES, which is why B2's box centre is at
  x = 1.85 and not 1.80. Every wall here is 0.1 m thick and the LiDAR only
  ever sees the FACE turned toward it, so a face is 0.05 m nearer than its
  box's centre line: B1's end face is at (1.0, -3.95), B2's near face at
  x = 1.80, and the measured gap is 0.80 m while the two centre lines are
  0.85 m apart. The same effect puts DOOR A's line, and so the origin of its
  door_<id> frame, at x = 2.45 rather than 2.50; its WIDTH is unaffected
  (0.90 m exactly), because that gap is between two box EDGES along y and the
  thickness is in x.

  WHAT THE SCENE DELIBERATELY ALSO CONTAINS, as distractors every gate should
  reject: the two wide mouths of the x = 2.5 partition (3.0 m and 4.0 m, both
  over width_max); four outer corners (gap 0, under width_min); and two
  genuine near misses, A1's end against B1's plane and B2's end against B1's
  plane, both exactly 1.00 m, which is INSIDE the width band but with the foot
  OUTSIDE B1's extent, so both are foot_outside. The detector should report
  exactly two doors in this world and nothing else.

  Walls are 1.0 m tall and 0.1 m thick, as in empty_room.sdf: the planar LiDAR
  sits well below the top, and the jambs are the box edges, so the measured
  widths are the nominal ones.
-->
<sdf version="1.6">
  <world name="test_room_doors">

    <physics name="1ms" type="ignored">
      <max_step_size>0.001</max_step_size>
      <real_time_factor>1.0</real_time_factor>
    </physics>

    <plugin filename="gz-sim-physics-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Physics"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-user-commands-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::UserCommands"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-scene-broadcaster-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::SceneBroadcaster"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-contact-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Contact"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-imu-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Imu"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-sensors-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Sensors">
      <render_engine>ogre2</render_engine>
    </plugin>

    <light type="directional" name="sun">
      <cast_shadows>true</cast_shadows>
      <pose>0 0 10 0 0 0</pose>
      <diffuse>0.8 0.8 0.8 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</specular>
      <attenuation>
        <range>1000</range>
        <constant>0.9</constant>
        <linear>0.01</linear>
        <quadratic>0.001</quadratic>
      </attenuation>
      <direction>-0.5 0.1 -0.9</direction>
    </light>

    <model name="ground_plane">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry>
            <plane><normal>0 0 1</normal><size>100 100</size></plane>
          </geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry>
            <plane><normal>0 0 1</normal><size>100 100</size></plane>
          </geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.8 0.8 0.8 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.8 0.8 0.8 1</diffuse>
            <specular>0.8 0.8 0.8 1</specular>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- The outer room: x in [-5, 7], y in [-6, 6]. -->
    <model name="room_walls">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="walls">
        <!-- OUT_nx: x = -5, along y -->
        <collision name="c_out_nx">
          <pose>-5 0 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.1 12 1</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="v_out_nx">
          <pose>-5 0 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.1 12 1</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.6 0.6 0.6 1</ambient><diffuse>0.6 0.6 0.6 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
        <!-- OUT_px: x = +7, along y -->
        <collision name="c_out_px">
          <pose>7 0 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.1 12 1</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="v_out_px">
          <pose>7 0 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.1 12 1</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.6 0.6 0.6 1</ambient><diffuse>0.6 0.6 0.6 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
        <!-- OUT_py: y = +6, along x -->
        <collision name="c_out_py">
          <pose>1 6 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>12 0.1 1</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="v_out_py">
          <pose>1 6 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>12 0.1 1</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.6 0.6 0.6 1</ambient><diffuse>0.6 0.6 0.6 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
        <!-- OUT_ny: y = -6, along x -->
        <collision name="c_out_ny">
          <pose>1 -6 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>12 0.1 1</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="v_out_ny">
          <pose>1 -6 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>12 0.1 1</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.6 0.6 0.6 1</ambient><diffuse>0.6 0.6 0.6 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!--
      DOOR A (case A): the x = 2.5 partition, in two collinear pieces.
      A1 spans y in [-3.00, -0.45] (2.55 m), A2 spans y in [+0.45, +2.00]
      (1.55 m). The gap between them is 0.90 m, centred on (2.5, 0.0), dead
      ahead of the spawn. Both pieces are well over the tracker's minimum wall
      span, so each is confirmed as its own track rather than merged across the
      gap (the collinear merge limit IS door_width_min_m, 0.70 m).
    -->
    <model name="case_a_partition">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="walls">
        <collision name="c_a1">
          <pose>2.5 -1.725 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.1 2.55 1</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="v_a1">
          <pose>2.5 -1.725 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.1 2.55 1</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.55 0.55 0.65 1</ambient><diffuse>0.55 0.55 0.65 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
        <collision name="c_a2">
          <pose>2.5 1.225 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.1 1.55 1</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="v_a2">
          <pose>2.5 1.225 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.1 1.55 1</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.55 0.55 0.65 1</ambient><diffuse>0.55 0.55 0.65 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!--
      DOOR B (case B): B1 runs along x at y = -4.0 over x in [-2.0, +1.0] and
      ENDS at (1.0, -4.0). B2 is perpendicular to it, along y at x = 1.8 over
      y in [-6.0, -3.0]. The perpendicular from B1's end onto B2's plane is
      0.80 m; its foot (1.8, -4.0) lies 1.0 m inside B2's extent, which is
      what makes this an opening rather than a corner.
    -->
    <model name="case_b_alcove">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="walls">
        <collision name="c_b1">
          <pose>-0.5 -4 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>3.0 0.1 1</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="v_b1">
          <pose>-0.5 -4 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>3.0 0.1 1</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.65 0.55 0.55 1</ambient><diffuse>0.65 0.55 0.55 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
        <collision name="c_b2">
          <pose>1.85 -4.5 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.1 3.0 1</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="v_b2">
          <pose>1.85 -4.5 0.5 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.1 3.0 1</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.65 0.55 0.55 1</ambient><diffuse>0.65 0.55 0.55 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

  </world>
</sdf>
